{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/statusview at f38c0b92375a13e30aaf0299d264ab280a0ae79e, the implementation report for request 099e4c83 under promise fc6c8720.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD DOES. statusview.Text folded whitespace and capped bytes and passed everything else through, so any actor could put an escape sequence, a bell, a bidi override or an invisible joiner into a request, report, artifact path or chat message and every reader's terminal would execute it. Text now escapes the C0 and C1 controls, DEL, the Unicode format characters and invalid UTF-8 as visible \\xNN and \\uNNNN, and the byte cap truncates on a token boundary so it cannot split an escape. New Safe does the same without the one-line fold or the cap, keeping newline and tab and escaping carriage return, for text a caller renders whole.\n\nCONDITIONS MET. Started from current main de30e088. Durable bytes and JSON meaning are preserved: nothing in the log changes, only what a bounded view shows. Every caller of the shared path is covered, and I did not take that on trust -- a test walks every string reachable through Build, BuildActorStatus, BuildWait, BuildOrientation, BuildWorkPage, BuildArtifactPage, BuildReviewGate and BuildStalenessWave from a projection whose every actor-controlled field is hostile. It found five callers nobody had neutralized: chat frame text, actor kind in both the status and orientation views, artifact row paths, the echoed path query, and the staleness path echo. Request conditions and three attempt reasons were passing through too. All are closed. Byte and rune caps stay valid; the escaped-cap test proves the truncation lands on a token boundary.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE. Eleven mutations, one per neutralization site plus the two escape switches and the cap boundary, each applied alone: 11 of 11 caught, each naming the field it broke. A green suite would have proved nothing here, so I broke every seam and watched it fail.\n\nTHE GOLDEN CHANGED, DELIBERATELY. internal/statusview/testdata/bounded-summary-golden.json shrank by 400 bytes. That test pins bounded output byte for byte, and its own comment anticipated this change: \"the day this text is escaped rather than merely trimmed, this test says whether the escaping moved with it.\" I recaptured the bytes from the escaping implementation and verified the new golden contains no control byte and carries the escapes as visible text. The property it pins -- selection before shaping, exact output -- is unchanged. A reviewer should check that recapture rather than take my word for it.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt clean, go vet clean, go test ./... clean, go test ./internal/docset/... clean at 73.8s, working tree clean, branch request/terminal-control-neutralization pushed to origin.\n\nThe layer contract does not change, so docs/reference/architecture.md is untouched. This head also changes cmd/gs by one line; that path carries its own artifact.","body":{"commit":"f38c0b92375a13e30aaf0299d264ab280a0ae79e","path":"internal/statusview"}}